# Per-machine defaults for submit_tob.sh. Copy to site.conf on the machine that
# needs it; submit_tob.sh sources it if present and ignores it if not, so nothing
# here has to work anywhere else.
#
#   cp site.conf.example site.conf   # then edit for this machine
#
# It answers "which interpreter, which modules" -- facts about the machine, not
# about the experiment, and the same for every run on it. Retyping them on each
# submission is how one submission ends up without them, which does not announce
# itself: the jobs run under whatever interpreter the compute nodes resolve and
# write shards indistinguishable from the rest.
#
# Sourced before the command line is parsed, so --python and --modules still win.
# Whatever is set here is printed before the run and recorded in run.meta.
#
# Keep it to these two variables. Anything a job needs in its own environment
# goes in site_env.sh, which is sourced on the compute node (see
# site_env.sh.example) -- but for a module-provided python, MODULES below is
# enough and site_env.sh is not needed at all.

# --- interpreter ------------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute path to a python that can import treeswift, on storage the compute
# nodes can see. Not a bare name: a name is resolved against the PATH of whatever
# machine looks it up, and the machine that submits is not the machine that runs.
#
# Leave unset to take $PROJECT_ROOT/.venv/bin/python when it exists, and python3
# from PATH otherwise.
#
# PYTHON_OPT=/fs/cbcb-lab/ekmolloy/jameslu/.venv/bin/python

# --- modules ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Space-separated modulefiles, loaded before the checks here and again inside
# every job. Needed when the interpreter above came from a module: a venv built
# against one still resolves its shared libraries through it.
#
# Pin the versions. `module avail` on a login node lists them, and an unpinned
# name lets a site upgrade change interpreters halfway through a sweep. Note
# that a version visible under /opt or /usr/local is not necessarily a module,
# and not necessarily present on the compute nodes.
#
# MODULES='Python3/3.11.11'
